<div class="help-article">
    <p class="help-lead">
        The <a href="tax">Tax report</a> gathers the year's transactions that may support deductions and credits on your T1
        return, grouped by the CRA line they relate to, with their receipts. You decide what's claimed; LoonieBooks keeps
        the record.
    </p>
    <p class="help-caution">
        It's a record-keeping aid, not tax advice. Eligibility, limits and thresholds are set by the CRA, and you still need the
        official receipts and slips. Line numbers were checked for a particular tax year; the report says when the year you're
        viewing is newer.
    </p>

    <h3>How transactions get onto it</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Suggested on import.</strong> Pharmacy, dental, daycare, donations, RRSP contributions and similar transactions are
            suggested automatically. Suggestions are deliberately broad, so plenty won't be claimable; they wait for you to
            confirm or reject them.</li>
        <li><strong>Chosen by you.</strong> Any transaction's <span class="help-ui">Tax</span> column on <a href="transactions">Transactions</a>
            sets its tax treatment, or <span class="help-ui">Set tax treatment of all … shown</span> sets it for a whole filtered list.
            Changing one offers the same for the account's other transactions in that category.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Tax categories include medical expenses (line 33099), charitable donations (34900), political contributions, child care
        (21400), moving (21900), RRSP (20800) and FHSA (20805) contributions, union and professional dues (21200), carrying
        charges (22100), support payments, employment expenses (T777), disability supports, home accessibility, tuition,
        student loan interest, educator supplies, self-employment expenses (T2125) and rental property expenses (T776).
    </p>

    <h3>Reading the report</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Pick the <strong>tax year</strong> at the top. Until the end of April it opens on last year, the one you're filing.</li>
        <li>The tiles show the <strong>Confirmed</strong> total, how many suggestions are <strong>Waiting for review</strong>, and how many
            confirmed claims have <strong>Missing receipts</strong>.</li>
        <li>Each tax category gets a card with its CRA line, its confirmed total, what's still to review, and a short note on what it
            covers. Open its transaction list to <span class="help-ui">Claim it</span>, mark <span class="help-ui">Not claimable</span>,
            <span class="help-ui">Remove</span> a claim, or attach a receipt.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Review … suggestion(s)</span> goes through them one at a time; see
            <a href="help?a=tax-review">Reviewing tax suggestions</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <HelpClip Name="tax-report" Caption="Claiming a dental bill on the Medical expenses card, then opening the printable summary." />

    <h3>Receipts</h3>
    <p>
        Each claim has an attach button for a PDF or photo of its receipt (up to 5 MB). Receipts are stored with your ledger and
        listed on the printable summary. To photograph a paper receipt and have LoonieBooks find its transaction, use
        <a href="receipts/scan">Scan receipt</a>; see <a href="help?a=receipts">Receipts</a>.
    </p>

